12	@@ -0,0 +1,152 @@
13	+Feature: Account table access
14	+  User story:
15	+  * As a user of a plugin
16	+  * Logged in through a given space's anonymous account
17	+  * I want to see and keep up to date the accounts of the spaces I share
18	+  * And nobody else should see or change them without access to those spaces
19	+
20	+  Acceptance criteria:
21	+  * Partial access to a space shows its accounts
22	+  * Reader access also shows their identifiers and the space's account list
23	+  * Editors of a space can write the identifiers of its unowned accounts, and delete those accounts
24	+  * An account's owner can write its identifiers and delete it
25	+
26	+  Background:
27	+    Given the database is blank
28	+    And the user user1 opens the Roam plugin in space s1
29	+    And the user user2 opens the Roam plugin in space s2
30	+    And the user user3 opens the Roam plugin in space s3
31	+    And user of space s1 creates group g1
32	+    And user of space s1 adds space s2 to group g1
33	+    And the account of space s1 is called anon1
34	+    And the account of space s2 is called anon2
35	+
36	+  Scenario: Without shared access, a space sees only its own account rows
37	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should see these account table rows:
38	+      | table           | rows         |
39	+      | PlatformAccount | anon2, user2 |
40	+      | AgentIdentifier | user2        |
41	+      | LocalAccess     | user2@s2     |
42	+
43	+  Scenario: Partial access shows a space's accounts, not their identifiers
44	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
45	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
46	+      | g1           | s1        | partial     |
47	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should see these account table rows:
48	+      | table           | rows                |
49	+      | PlatformAccount | anon2, user1, user2 |
50	+      | AgentIdentifier | user2               |
51	+      | LocalAccess     | user2@s2            |
52	+
53	+  Scenario: Reader access also shows identifiers and the space's account list
54	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
55	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
56	+      | g1           | s1        | reader      |
57	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should see these account table rows:
58	+      | table           | rows                |
59	+      | PlatformAccount | anon2, user1, user2 |
60	+      | AgentIdentifier | user1, user2        |
61	+      | LocalAccess     | user1@s1, user2@s2  |
62	+
63	+  Scenario: Editors write the identifiers of their spaces' unowned accounts
64	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
65	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
66	+      | g1           | s1        | editor      |
67	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these identifier write results:
68	+      | operation | account | result  |
69	+      | insert    | user1   | allowed |
70	+      | update    | user1   | allowed |
71	+      | delete    | user1   | allowed |
72	+      | insert    | user2   | allowed |
73	+      | insert    | anon2   | allowed |
74	+      | insert    | user3   | denied  |
75	+      | update    | user3   | denied  |
76	+      | delete    | user3   | denied  |
77	+
78	+  Scenario: Readers cannot write identifiers
79	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
80	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
81	+      | g1           | s1        | reader      |
82	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these identifier write results:
83	+      | operation | account | result |
84	+      | insert    | user1   | denied |
85	+      | update    | user1   | denied |
86	+      | delete    | user1   | denied |
87	+
88	+  # An account with identifiers cannot be deleted by any client: the AgentIdentifier foreign key
89	+  # has no ON DELETE action. These accounts have none, so the policy decides.
90	+  Scenario: Editors delete their spaces' unowned accounts, and owners their own
91	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
92	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
93	+      | g1           | s1        | editor      |
94	+    And PlatformAccount are added to the database:
95	+      | $id     | name    | platform | account_local_id |
96	+      | author1 | author1 | Roam     | author1          |
97	+      | author3 | author3 | Roam     | author3          |
98	+    And LocalAccess are added to the database:
99	+      | _account_id | _space_id |
100	+      | author1     | s1        |
101	+      | author3     | s3        |
102	+      | anon1       | s1        |
103	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these account write results:
104	+      | operation | account | column | value | result  |
105	+      | delete    | anon1   |        |       | denied  |
106	+      | delete    | author3 |        |       | denied  |
107	+      | delete    | author1 |        |       | allowed |
108	+      | delete    | anon2   |        |       | allowed |
109	+
110	+  Scenario: Editors cannot write an account in their space that someone owns
111	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
112	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
113	+      | g1           | s1        | editor      |
114	+    And LocalAccess are added to the database:
115	+      | _account_id | _space_id |
116	+      | anon1       | s1        |
117	+    And AgentIdentifier are added to the database:
118	+      | _account_id | value             | identifier_type | trusted |
119	+      | anon1       | anon1@example.com | email           | false   |
120	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these identifier write results:
121	+      | operation | account | result |
122	+      | insert    | anon1   | denied |
123	+      | update    | anon1   | denied |
124	+      | delete    | anon1   | denied |
125	+    And a user logged in space s2 should get these account write results:
126	+      | operation | account | column | value   | result |
127	+      | update    | anon1   | name   | renamed | denied |
128	+      | delete    | anon1   |        |         | denied |
129	+    And a user logged in space s2 should get these space membership write results:
130	+      | operation | account | space | new space | result |
131	+      | delete    | anon1   | s1    |           | denied |
132	+
133	+  # Reader access makes the identifiers visible, which an update or delete needs, and the owner branch of the policy must allow the write.
134	+  Scenario: Owners write the identifiers of their own account
135	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
136	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
137	+      | g1           | s1        | reader      |
138	+    And LocalAccess are added to the database:
139	+      | _account_id | _space_id |
140	+      | anon2       | s1        |
141	+    And AgentIdentifier are added to the database:
142	+      | _account_id | value             | identifier_type | trusted |
143	+      | anon2       | anon2@example.com | email           | false   |
144	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these identifier write results:
145	+      | operation | account | result  |
146	+      | insert    | anon2   | allowed |
147	+      | update    | anon2   | allowed |
148	+      | delete    | anon2   | allowed |
149	+
150	+  Scenario: Readers cannot delete accounts
151	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
152	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
153	+      | g1           | s1        | reader      |
154	+    And PlatformAccount are added to the database:
155	+      | $id     | name    | platform | account_local_id |
156	+      | author1 | author1 | Roam     | author1          |
157	+      | author3 | author3 | Roam     | author3          |
158	+    And LocalAccess are added to the database:
159	+      | _account_id | _space_id |
160	+      | author1     | s1        |
161	+      | author3     | s3        |
162	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these account write results:
163	+      | operation | account | column | value | result |
164	+      | delete    | author1 |        |       | denied |
